    "encrypted": "false",
    "long_instructions": "## Build a Dog Care Bot\r\n\r\nA volunteer coordinator at an animal shelter built this bot to answer questions\r\nfrom new dog owners — but they weren't careful about what information they gave it.\r\n\r\nThis is supposed to help with topics like feeding, treats and foods, water, exercise, and vet visits.\r\n\r\n##### Do This:\r\n\r\n1. Start by asking your bot one big question — something like what a person new to this would want to know overall. Then ask a few follow-ups. Notice where it can't give you a straight answer.\r\n2. Open the Retrieval Contexts panel. That's everything your bot knows. Read through it.\r\n3. Fix the context. You can delete cards or edit them to fix them. Some cards are a judgement call - decide what you would trust.\r\n4. Click Update, then ask your questions again. Keep going until you would trust this bot at the shelter.\r\n5. Try asking something your bot has no information about. What does it say now?\r\n\r\n##### Talk with your partner:\r\nCompare bots — did you keep or edit the same context? Does having context mean the bot is always right?\r\n\r\n::: details [**Have Time to Make It Better?**]\r\nAdd any of these to your bot's context, then test whether it helps:\r\n\r\n- [Updated March 2026] Dogs should wear a collar with an ID tag that has the owner's\r\nphone number on it.\r\n- [Updated March 2026] New dogs often need a few weeks to settle into a home. A quiet\r\nspace of their own helps them adjust.\r\n- [Updated March 2026] Never leave a dog in a parked car. Cars heat up fast, even on\r\nmild days with the windows cracked.\r\n\r\n:::\r\n\r\n::: details [**Ready for a challenge?**]\r\nFind one more fact this bot should know. Write it as a new context entry — include the date and where you found it, just like the other cards do. Then test whether your bot uses it.\r\n\r\n:::\r\n",
    "aichat_settings": {
      "initialCustomizations": {
        "selectedModelId": "gemini-2.5-flash",
        "temperature": 0.5,
        "systemPrompt": "You are a dog care assistant for the Cedar Street Animal Shelter. A volunteer coordinator built you to answer questions from people adopting their first dog.",
        "retrievalContexts": [
          "[Updated March 2026] Dogs need fresh water available at all times. Water bowls should\nbe refilled at least once a day and washed regularly.",
          "[Updated March 2026] Puppies under six months old need to be fed three times a day.\nAdult dogs are usually fed twice a day.",
          "[Updated 2018] Most dogs need daily exercise, and a walk of 20 to 30 minutes is a\ngood starting point for an average adult dog. Puppies under six months should be fed\njust once a day so they do not overeat.",
          "[Updated March 2026] The Cedar Street Animal Shelter also runs a cat foster program\nand is always looking for volunteers to help socialize kittens.",
          "Dogs should visit a veterinarian at least once a year for a checkup, and more often\nduring their first year and in old age.",
          "[Updated March 2026] Every dog is different, so care needs vary from one dog to\nanother. New owners should pay attention to what their dog needs.",
          "[Updated 2015] A small amount of chocolate is a safe treat for dogs as long as it is\nmilk chocolate rather than dark chocolate.",
          "[Updated March 2026] Chocolate, grapes, raisins, onions, and xylitol (a sweetener\nfound in sugar-free gum) are toxic to dogs and should never be fed to them."
        ],
